WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.421 --> 00:00:03.088
- [Narrator] Rarely do
nature and architecture

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:04.739
come together so perfectly,

00:00:04.739 --> 00:00:08.844
as the divine Greek
monasteries of Meteora.

00:00:08.844 --> 00:00:11.761
(orchestral music)

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:15.586
Meteora is a collection of
Greek Orthodox monasteries

00:00:15.586 --> 00:00:19.963
perched on rock pillars
1,300 feet above ground.

00:00:19.963 --> 00:00:21.045
They were built by Greek monks

00:00:21.045 --> 00:00:23.376
from the 14th to the 16th centuries.

00:00:23.376 --> 00:00:25.216
They were constructed by
using a network of ropes,

00:00:25.216 --> 00:00:26.957
pulleys, baskets, and ladders,

00:00:26.957 --> 00:00:30.888
which lifted supplies
up the dramatic cliffs.

00:00:30.888 --> 00:00:32.048
In addition to solitude,

00:00:32.048 --> 00:00:33.796
the location brought the monks and locals

00:00:33.796 --> 00:00:36.496
a place of protection
from unwanted trespassers

00:00:36.496 --> 00:00:39.106
and a sanctuary during
invasions from the Turks.

00:00:39.106 --> 00:00:42.517
Today, only six of the
original 20 monasteries remain,

00:00:42.517 --> 00:00:44.677
but they are still inhabited
by 20 monks and four nuns

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:47.426
who are full-time residents.

00:00:47.426 --> 00:00:49.368
The Meteora is one of
only a handful of places

00:00:49.368 --> 00:00:51.677
that are listed as a
UNESCO World Heritage Site

00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:55.056
under two categories: nature and art.

00:00:55.056 --> 00:00:55.889
Without changing the landscape,

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.778
the monks managed to build monasteries

00:00:57.778 --> 00:01:00.938
that seem like extensions
of the mountain itself.

00:01:00.938 --> 00:01:04.138
The name Meteora translates
to suspended in space.

00:01:04.138 --> 00:01:08.206
And these monasteries
truly seem to defy gravity.

00:01:08.206 --> 00:01:10.789
(upbeat music)

00:01:12.578 --> 00:01:15.495
(electronic music)

